<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- TR-1.9 / FR-1.8.2: read body mass from Health. Read-only and on-device (G-5.4). -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- FR-1.12.1: THE SWITCH. ModelConfiguration.cloudKitDatabase defaults to `.automatic`,
	     which means "mirror if the process has this entitlement" — so this key, not any line of
	     Swift, is what makes mirroring possible at all. scripts/check-cloudkit.sh still bans it
	     everywhere else in the repo and names this file as one of exactly two exemptions; its
	     check 5 fails if this key or the container below ever goes missing while the exemption
	     stays. The container string is duplicated in that script on purpose — the point is that
	     the two must agree, and a check reading its expectation from the file it checks would
	     agree with anything. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>iCloud.lockw1n.Attempt</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-services</key>
	<array>
		<string>CloudKit</string>
	</array>
	<!-- T-1.86 / DOD-1.4: WHICH DATABASE A BUILD REACHES, and the only reason this key exists.
	     Without it the environment comes from how the build was signed: a distribution-signed
	     build (TestFlight, App Store) reaches Production and a development-signed one — Xcode's
	     Run, on the phone or the simulator — reaches Development. Those are two separate
	     databases with two separate schemas, so two such builds never see each other's rows, and
	     DOD-1.4's "two devices" cannot be demonstrated with the phone on TestFlight and the
	     simulator on Xcode. Pinning it to Production is what makes them one device pair.

	     THE PRICE, stated because it is not recoverable by deleting an app: every Debug build now
	     writes to the lifter's real private database. Anything a development build creates —
	     a restored fixture, a synthetic store, a scratch session — mirrors to the real account
	     and is not purged by uninstalling. Restoring a scale backup with sync ON is the specific
	     way to lose an afternoon to it; turn sync off in Settings first (T-1.83's state E).

	     Production is additive-only, which is the other half of the price: a record type or field
	     that reaches it can never be deleted, only Development can be reset. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-environment</key>
	<string>Production</string>
</dict>
</plist>
